{
  "id": "80714",
  "type": "Page",
  "tags": [],
  "title": "Homogeneous and Heterogeneous Catalysts",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "knunnf3cd3j164p",
                "text": "Homogeneous Catalysts"
              }
            ],
            "id": "i1ak1zymqdmqxsd",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "homogeneous catalyst"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A catalyst present in the same phase as the reactants."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3695011666",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2863610275",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is present in the same phase as the reactants. It interacts with a reactant to form an intermediate substance, which then decomposes or reacts with another reactant in one or more steps to regenerate the original catalyst and form product."
              }
            ],
            "id": "03s2a0l11avoqgy",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "As an important illustration of homogeneous catalysis, consider Earth’s ozone layer. Ozone in the upper atmosphere, which protects Earth from ultraviolet radiation, is formed when oxygen molecules absorb ultraviolet light and undergo the reaction:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "6s10wxfl6wrvh9v",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "t9sg6bqb54udp77",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "e91zy5tl49z0p0t",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "3\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;\\;\\xrightarrow{h\\nu}\\;\\;2\\;{\\mathrm O}_3(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Ozone is a relatively unstable molecule that decomposes to yield diatomic oxygen by the reverse of this equation. This decomposition reaction is consistent with the following mechanism:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "kpisdmewf557kiw",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "hr27yh00cpggih3",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "pnz4307hvrbtfq3",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{l}{\\mathrm O}_3\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm O}_2\\;+\\;\\mathrm O\\\\\\mathrm O\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_3\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;2\\;{\\mathrm O}_2\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The presence of nitric oxide, NO, influences the rate of decomposition of ozone. Nitric oxide acts as a catalyst in the following mechanism:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "879p0mqyegbvg86",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "zcrijn46mrb0z1p",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "45osq6ma0pfj1sl",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "\\begin{array}{l}\\mathrm{NO}(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_3(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm{NO}}_2(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\\\{\\mathrm O}_3(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\;+\\;\\mathrm O(g)\\\\{\\mathrm{NO}}_2(g)\\;+\\;\\mathrm O(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;\\mathrm{NO}(g)\\;+\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)\\end{array}",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The overall chemical change for the catalyzed mechanism is the same as:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ofl37weru4987fr",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "6ujz63mp7d5ukfq",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ophigytga8v1q9g",
            "legacyBlockRendered": "true",
            "src": "2\\;{\\mathrm O}_3(g)\\;\\;\\longrightarrow\\;\\;3\\;{\\mathrm O}_2(g)",
            "subtype": "latex",
            "type": "formula"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The nitric oxide reacts and is regenerated in these reactions. It is not permanently used up; thus, it acts as a catalyst. The rate of decomposition of ozone is greater in the presence of nitric oxide because of the catalytic activity of NO. Certain compounds that contain chlorine also catalyze the decomposition of ozone."
              }
            ],
            "id": "so9eioyxun2ipak",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "es3vp18y9sdupn2",
                "text": "Heterogeneous Catalysts"
              }
            ],
            "id": "tptgjtyvttjdl9m",
            "type": "h1"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "A "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "heterogeneous catalyst"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A catalyst present in a different phase from the reactants, furnishing a surface at which a reaction can occur."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1303801080",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2691612313",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is a catalyst that is present in a different phase (usually a solid) than the reactants. Such catalysts generally function by furnishing an active surface upon which a reaction can occur. Gas- and liquid-phase reactions catalyzed by heterogeneous catalysts occur on the surface of the catalyst rather than within the gas or liquid phase."
              }
            ],
            "id": "wpnmb5wxcdtmwin",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Heterogeneous "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "catalysis"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "The increase in rate of a chemical reaction due to the addition of a catalyst."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3379675063",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "4152451891",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " has at least four steps:"
              }
            ],
            "id": "ealdw2qw5iu41v0",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "Adsorption"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "A process in which atoms, molecules, or ions of a liquid, gas, or dissolved solid adhere to a surface."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "3000572210",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "52925484",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " of the reactant onto the surface of the catalyst"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3874482465",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "dlaiwv6uac06eli",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Activation of the adsorbed reactant"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3944089391",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "khrtr2d063zj4e3",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Reaction of the adsorbed reactant"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "2310773624",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "dijdzs1o5lhy714",
                "type": "li"
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": ""
                      },
                      {
                        "children": [
                          {
                            "text": "Diffusion"
                          }
                        ],
                        "content": [
                          {
                            "children": [
                              {
                                "text": "The movement of atoms or molecules from a region of relatively high concentration to one of relatively low concentration."
                              }
                            ],
                            "id": "1116195867",
                            "type": "p"
                          }
                        ],
                        "id": "1754706915",
                        "trigger": "hover",
                        "type": "popup"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " of the product from the surface into the gas or liquid phase (desorption)"
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1825767953",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "ox0kpd4ba826usg",
                "type": "li"
              }
            ],
            "id": "m4f2yzfrar9o6rk",
            "style": "decimal",
            "type": "ol"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Any one of these steps may be slow and thus may serve as the rate-determining step. In general, however, in the presence of the catalyst, the overall rate of the reaction is faster than it would be if the reactants were in the gas or liquid phase."
              }
            ],
            "id": "uifc3d543gaqoip",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The figure below illustrates the steps that chemists believe to occur in the reaction of compounds containing a carbon–carbon double bond with hydrogen on a nickel catalyst. Nickel is the catalyst used in the hydrogenation of polyunsaturated fats and oils (which contain several carbon–carbon double bonds) to produce saturated fats and oils (which contain only carbon–carbon single bonds)."
              }
            ],
            "id": "vnwq3g749a9ticb",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "alt": "In this figure, four diagrams labeled a through d are shown. In each, a green square surface is shown in perspective to provide a three-dimensional appearance. In a, the label “N i surface” is placed above with a line segment extending to the green square. At the lower left and upper right, pairs of white spheres bonded tougher together appear as well as white spheres on the green surface. Black arrows are drawn from each of the white spheres above the surface to the white sphere on the green surface. In b, the white spheres are still present on the green surface. Near the center of this surface is a molecule with two central black spheres with a double bond indicated by two horizontal black rods between them. Above and below to the left and right, a total of four white spheres are connected to the black spheres with white rods. A line segment extends from this structure to the label, “Ethylene absorbed on surface breaking pi bonds.” Just above this is a nearly identical structure greyed out with three downward pointing arrows to the black and white structure to indicate downward motion. The label “Ethylene” at the top of the diagram is connected to the greyed out structure with a line segment. In c, the diagram is very similar to b except that the greyed out structure and labels are gone and one of the white spheres near the black and white structure in each pair on the green surface is greyed out. Arrows point from the greyed out white spheres to the double bond between the two black spheres. In d, only a single white sphere remains from each pair in the green surface. A curved arrow points from the middle of the green surface to a model above with two central black spheres with a single black rod indicating a single bond between them. Each of the black rods has three small white spheres bonded as indicated by white rods between the black spheres and the small white spheres. The four bonds around each black sphere are evenly distributed about the black spheres.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "There are four steps in the catalysis of the reaction C"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "4"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " + H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " ⟶ C"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " by nickel. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(a)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Hydrogen is adsorbed on the surface, breaking the H–H bonds and forming Ni–H bonds. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(b)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Ethylene is adsorbed on the surface, breaking the π-bond and forming Ni–C bonds. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(c)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " Atoms diffuse across the surface and form new C–H bonds when they collide. "
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "strong": true,
                    "text": "(d)"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " C"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "2"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "H"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "sub": true,
                    "text": "6"
                  },
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": " molecules escape from the nickel surface because they are not strongly attracted to nickel."
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "3a796q4evnyk1ay",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "4ma66m3axb11uoi",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/2c/2c8f8e7a3467f7f0cc8b1e6f6f4c1be0/CNX_Chem_12_07_HetCats.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "650"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Other significant industrial processes that involve the use of heterogeneous catalysts include the preparation of sulfuric acid, the preparation of ammonia, the oxidation of ammonia to nitric acid, and the synthesis of methanol, CH"
              },
              {
                "sub": true,
                "text": "3"
              },
              {
                "text": "OH. Heterogeneous catalysts are also used in the catalytic converters found on most gasoline-powered automobiles."
              }
            ],
            "id": "cltre7got096898",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
